# Guide for AI coding assistants

This is a Go API generated by apistock. Read [ARCHITECTURE.md](ARCHITECTURE.md) before changing code.

## Commands

| Purpose | Command |
|---|---|
| Run locally: services, migrations, seed data, reload | `aps dev` |
| Start PostgreSQL and Mailpit | `docker compose up -d --wait` |
| Apply migrations | `go run ./cmd/migrate` |
| Seed a development database (`admin@example.com`, password printed once) | `go run ./cmd/seed` |
| Test (must pass before finishing) | `APISTOCK_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://⟦.Name⟧:⟦.Name⟧@127.0.0.1:5432/⟦.Name⟧?sslmode=disable go test ./...` |
| Vet | `go vet ./...` |
| Run the API only | `go run ./cmd/api` |
| Update the API contract after changing endpoints | `go run ./cmd/api openapi > api/openapi.json` |
| Give an account a platform role | `go run ./cmd/api grant-role <email> <role>` (`go run ./cmd/api roles` lists them) |

## Where code goes

| Change | Location |
|---|---|
| Business rule or domain error | `internal/modules/<module>/domain/` |
| Application logic | `internal/modules/<module>/usecase/` |
| Interface for storage or external services | `internal/modules/<module>/usecase/ports.go` |
| SQL query | `internal/modules/<module>/repository/<operation>.go` (hand-written SQL, one file per operation) |
| HTTP endpoint | `internal/modules/<module>/delivery/` (`huma.Register`) |
| Domain error → HTTP status and error code | `internal/app/module_<module>.go` |
| Secret or infrastructure setting | `internal/app/config.go` and `.env.example` |
| Tunable operators change at runtime | `internal/app/settings.go` (a runtime setting, not an env var) |
| Background job | `aps gen job <Name> --yes [--schedule SPEC \| --every D \| --on-demand] [--timeout D] [--max-attempts N]`: creates `internal/jobs/<name>/`, `internal/app/job_<name>.go` and one line in `internal/app/jobs.go` |
| Database change outside a new resource (column, index, table) | `aps gen migration <name> --yes`, then write the SQL under `-- +goose Up` before `go run ./cmd/migrate` (an empty migration is recorded as applied; never edit a released migration) |
| Sign-up, sign-in or session rule | `internal/modules/auth/usecase/` (flows) and `internal/modules/auth/repository/` (SQL); security helpers come from `apistock.dev/modules/auth` |
| Permission or platform role | `internal/app/permissions.go`; check it in the use case with `actor.Can(permission)` |
| Send an email | Call the `mail.Sender` built as `mailer` in `internal/app/app.go` (pass it to the module); leave `From` empty so the `mail.*` settings apply |
| Email provider (Resend or SMTP) | `aps add mail --provider resend\|smtp --yes`; never edit `internal/app/infra_mail.go` or the `# aps:begin mail` block by hand. Secrets go in `.env` only |
| Email sender name, address, reply-to | Runtime settings `mail.from_name`, `mail.from_email`, `mail.reply_to` (not env vars) |
| New module | `internal/modules/<module>/`, `internal/app/module_<module>.go`, one line in `internal/app/modules.go` |
| New resource owned by users (CRUD with its own table) | `aps gen resource <Name> <field:type>... --yes` (types `string`, `string:unique`, `text`, `'enum(a,b)'`), then `go run ./cmd/migrate`; it creates a module like `internal/modules/projects/`. Keep the owner ID in every query and the cross-owner tests |

## Rules

- Keep `domain/` free of third-party imports and struct tags.
- Never read environment variables outside `internal/app`.
- Never import one module from another.
- Return domain errors from handlers; don't build HTTP errors in use cases.
- Request body structs start with ``_ struct{} `json:"-" additionalProperties:"true"` `` so unknown fields are ignored.
- Error codes, runtime setting keys and job names are public API: add new ones, never rename existing ones.
- Never put secrets in runtime settings or job arguments.
- Never log secrets, tokens, passwords or email addresses; log IDs.
- Never store passwords, session tokens or codes in plain text; use the auth library's `Hasher`, `NewToken`/`HashToken` and `NewCode`/`HashCode`.
- Never send email directly through a provider; use the mailer so it's queued, retried and goes to Mailpit in development.
- Keep `//aps:anchor` comments; apistock uses them to add modules and jobs.
